\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces High-level cloud-native microservices architecture of the platform: a Next.js front-end talks only to the Spring Cloud API Gateway, which routes authenticated traffic to six domain services, each owning a private PostgreSQL database, supported by a Eureka/Config control plane, Keycloak identity, a Kafka event backbone, Redis/RabbitMQ infrastructure, a Python/FastAPI ML service, and the external football-data.org feed.}}{74}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Docker Compose deployment view: a single Docker host runs every container on one shared bridge network, grouping the infrastructure backing services, the Spring Cloud control plane, the six Spring Boot domain services, the Python/FastAPI machine-learning services and the Next.js frontend.}}{75}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Use-case diagram for administration and club management, showing the Administrator, Sport Manager and Team Manager actors and the account, staffing, team, transfer, competition, roster, contract and Club Hub operations each performs.}}{76}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Use-case diagram for match and training operations, in which the coach drives training planning and the line-up-mandatory match and live-match workflows while the analyst authors match and player analyses and consumes the machine-learning match-outcome and player-rating predictions.}}{77}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Use-case diagram for the medical and fitness module, mapping the Doctor, Physiotherapist and Fitness Coach to the staged injury-to-recovery workflow and the shared medical catalogues.}}{78}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Use-case diagram for the scouting and sponsorship workflows alongside the read-only player and fan surfaces, where scouts browse prospects and author reports, sponsors submit and negotiate offers, and players and fans are confined to read-only views (FR-7, FR-9, FR-22).}}{79}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Class model of the user-management domain: the central \texttt {User} identity record and its one-to-one role-extension classes (\texttt {PlayerProfile}, \texttt {HeadCoachProfile}, \texttt {NationalTeam} and the remaining role extensions), realising the vertical-extension pattern of \texttt {mscms\_user}.}}{80}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Class model of the player-management domain: the Sport--Team--Roster composition chain, per-player PlayerContract, the external OuterTeam--OuterPlayer squad, and the directed, status-bearing transfer and national-team call-up associations.}}{80}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Class model of the training-match service, showing how a Match owns its MatchFormation, MatchEvents and PlayerMatchStatistics while the formation in turn owns the starting-eleven MatchLineup, and how a TrainingPlan groups TrainingSessions that own their drills, attendance and player assessments.}}{81}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Class model of the competitions domain: a Competition composes its participating CompetitionTeam rows and its ordered CompetitionFixture rows, whose round label separates the Champions League league phase from the knockout rounds.}}{82}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Class model of the medical aggregate: \texttt {Injury} is the aggregate root whose \texttt {status} drives the whole recovery journey, composing \texttt {Diagnosis}, \texttt {Treatment} and \texttt {Rehabilitation} (which in turn composes \texttt {RecoveryProgram}), while \texttt {FitnessTest} and \texttt {TrainingLoad} stand apart as independent measurement classes.}}{83}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Class model of the communication subsystem (\texttt {mscms\_notification}), showing per-recipient notifications, role-targeted alerts, threaded messages, and chat groups that compose their members.}}{84}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Class model of the reports-and-analytics domain (\texttt {mscms\_reports}): six authored or derived classes that aggregate operational data over a period and reference matches, players, teams and sponsors only by opaque identifier rather than by object links.}}{84}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.14}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram of the \texttt {mscms\_user} database, showing the common USER identity record and its one-to-one per-role profile extensions (PLAYER, HEAD\_COACH, NATIONAL\_TEAM, and the remaining role tables).}}{85}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.15}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram of the \texttt {mscms\_player} database, showing the club hierarchy (sports, teams, rosters), player contracts and transfer/call-up records, and the external scouting catalogue (outer teams and players).}}{86}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram of the \texttt {mscms\_training} database, showing the matches subsystem (formations, line-ups, events and per-player statistics), the database-backed competition subsystem (teams and fixtures), and the training subsystem (plans, drills and attendance) with their principal columns and foreign-key relationships.}}{87}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram for the \texttt {mscms\_medical} database, showing the staged injury journey (injuries owning diagnoses, treatments and rehabilitations, with rehabilitations driving recovery programmes) alongside the independent fitness-test and training-load records.}}{88}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.18}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram of the \texttt {mscms\_notification} database, showing notifications, alerts, and the team-chat groups, members, and threaded messages.}}{89}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.19}{\ignorespaces Entity-relationship diagram of the \texttt {mscms\_reports} database, whose analytical entities aggregate operational data from other domains through opaque cross-service references rather than foreign-key constraints.}}{90}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.20}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of the authentication flow: credentials submitted on the front-end traverse the gateway and the user-management service, which performs an OAuth~2.0 password-grant exchange at Keycloak to obtain and return the JWT access token and role.}}{91}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.21}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram for scheduling an official match: the coach builds the formation and starting eleven from a competition fixture, the training-match service validates the kick-off time and line-up, and on success persists the Match, MatchFormation and MatchLineup atomically in a single transaction so a match can never exist without a starting eleven.}}{91}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of live-match phase progression and goal recording, showing the broadcast phase machine (first half, half-time, second half, full-time) and the minute-bounding validation that rejects out-of-phase goals before persisting the event and updating the score.}}{92}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.23}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram for an in-match injury and the forced substitution it triggers, showing how training-match persists the INJURY match event while delegating to medical-fitness to create the real injury record before the operator records the substitution.}}{93}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.24}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of the injury-to-recovery workflow, advancing a player through diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation and a recovery programme, with the stage read from the single injury status and availability restored only on a passing fitness test.}}{94}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.25}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram for group messaging: the front-end opens a WebSocket to \texttt {/ws/chat} on the notification-mail service (outside the gateway) and, when the socket is available, sends a message that the handler persists as a \texttt {Message} keyed by \texttt {group\_id} and broadcasts to connected group members in real time; if the socket is unavailable the client falls back to authenticated HTTP polling of the group's messages.}}{95}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.26}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of scheduled external fixture ingestion: the training-match service pulls competition-scoped matches and standings from football-data.org, filters and de-duplicates fixtures before persisting them, reads standings through without storing, and lets the front-end consume the data from the platform's own model.}}{96}{figure.caption.53}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.27}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of event-driven notification delivery: the training-match service publishes a domain event to Kafka and returns immediately, while the notification-mail service asynchronously consumes the event, persists a notification, and later surfaces it to the recipient's top-bar bell.}}{96}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.28}{\ignorespaces Sequence diagram of the password-change flow, in which the user-management service first verifies the current password via an OAuth~2.0 password-grant exchange against Keycloak and only on success sets the new password through the Keycloak Admin API.}}{97}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.29}{\ignorespaces State machine for live-match phase progression, where a scheduled match advances through the regulation halves to full time and, for an unresolved knockout tie only, continues into extra time and a penalty shoot-out before finishing.}}{98}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.30}{\ignorespaces State machine for the injury lifecycle and recovery journey: the Injury.status field drives Active to Recovering to Recovered, with the staged journey (Diagnosis, Treatment, Rehabilitation, Recovery Program, Fitness Test) walked along the transitions, a failed fitness test looping back and a passed test restoring the player to Available.}}{98}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.31}{\ignorespaces State machine for the competition lifecycle: a competition moves from Draft through an editable Ongoing phase to a read-only Completed state once every fixture is played, with the Champions League specialisation in which the knockout bracket stays locked until the league phase is complete.}}{98}{figure.caption.58}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The Sportify sign-in screen. Credentials are validated against the Keycloak \texttt {mscms} realm through the API gateway, which returns a signed JWT that the front-end decodes to establish the user's identity and role.}}{102}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces The role-aware dashboard. Summary cards, a featured match and a mini league table aggregate data from multiple microservices into a single overview surface, with card visibility governed by the user's role.}}{103}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The Club Hub, presenting club identity, coaching and stadium details, the trophy cabinet, the squad, and results grouped by competition with a last-five form strip.}}{104}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The squad listing. Players are rendered as photograph-bearing cards, filterable by sport and availability, and served by the player-management service.}}{106}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces The expanded player profile, showing biography, contract and statistics for an individual squad member, opened with a subtle animation from the squad listing.}}{106}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces The Competitions landing page, with selectable tabs for La~Liga and the UEFA Champions League backed by persistent competition entities.}}{108}{figure.caption.80}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces The La~Liga competition view, showing the live standings table alongside the season's fixtures and results.}}{109}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces The UEFA Champions League knockout bracket, presenting the progression of ties from the round of sixteen to the final. The bracket is unlocked only once the league phase has concluded.}}{110}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces The guided competition-creation flow: preset selection, season selection and team selection culminate in a generated fixture set persisted by the training-match service.}}{111}{figure.caption.90}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces The Head-to-Head view, summarising the club's all-time record against each opponent (played, won, drawn, lost and goals) computed from the authoritative match records, with the underlying fixtures expandable for any opponent.}}{112}{figure.caption.93}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces The World Cup view, presenting the tournament's group tables and knockout bracket. Group standings and the bracket are computed on the server and served to the browser through the gateway via read-through endpoints, so the external data provider is never called from the client.}}{114}{figure.caption.97}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces The Trophy Room, presenting the club's honours as a curated cabinet grouped by competition and era, complementing the trophy summary on the Club Hub.}}{114}{figure.caption.100}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.13}{\ignorespaces The Match Hub, listing the club's matches with status and team filters and a schedule action. Upcoming cards display a live countdown and transition to a LIVE state at kickoff.}}{115}{figure.caption.103}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.14}{\ignorespaces Scheduling a match. The Friendly/Official chooser, opponent or fixture selection and a future-dated kickoff feed a draft that is only persisted once a lineup has been confirmed.}}{117}{figure.caption.106}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.15}{\ignorespaces The formation-first lineup builder. Players are positioned on the pitch by drag-and-drop, and the resulting formation and starting XI are persisted atomically with the match.}}{118}{figure.caption.110}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.16}{\ignorespaces The live-match engine. The broadcast-style phase machine, stoppage-time control, event entry, per-sport substitutions and in-match injury recording drive the match in real time and persist every action to the back-end.}}{121}{figure.caption.112}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.17}{\ignorespaces The match details page, presenting the reconstructed starting XI, the events timeline, match statistics and the post-match player-review wall for a completed match.}}{122}{figure.caption.113}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.18}{\ignorespaces The Medical Treatment Room, separating currently injured players from those who have recovered. Logging an injury automatically withdraws the player from matches, lineups and training until a passing fitness test restores availability.}}{123}{figure.caption.116}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.19}{\ignorespaces The per-player injury journey stepper, progressing from the initial injury through diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation, recovery and a fitness test to full recovery. The injury's status is the single source of truth for the current stage.}}{124}{figure.caption.119}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.20}{\ignorespaces The general medical catalogs. Reusable injury, diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation, recovery and fitness-test records double as templates that pre-fill the journey's stage forms.}}{125}{figure.caption.121}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.21}{\ignorespaces The team group chat. Group conversations, invitations, threaded replies and emoji reactions are delivered over a native WebSocket to the notification-mail service, with polling as a fallback.}}{126}{figure.caption.125}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.22}{\ignorespaces The notification centre opened from the top-bar bell. Alerts are generated automatically from Kafka domain events and from the live-match engine, tagged by role and unread state.}}{128}{figure.caption.129}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.23}{\ignorespaces The training schedule with a live-now indicator, showing plan-driven sessions with drills, attendance and completion tracking.}}{129}{figure.caption.132}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.24}{\ignorespaces The Scouting module, where scouts record prospect reports against structured fields for review by the sport manager.}}{130}{figure.caption.134}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.25}{\ignorespaces The Sponsors module, presenting sponsor offers and contract negotiation as structured, auditable records.}}{130}{figure.caption.136}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.26}{\ignorespaces The Reports and Analytics module, aggregating match, team, player and training data served by the reports-analytics service.}}{131}{figure.caption.138}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.27}{\ignorespaces The Settings module, with profile, preferences, notification and security tabs. Password changes are verified and applied through Keycloak.}}{132}{figure.caption.141}%
